This is actually a common misunderstanding, so let me explain how a professional project is usually organized.

Core folder

The core/ folder is your production code.

Everything works together.

TradingPipeline
      │
      ├── MarketStructure
      ├── FeatureEngineering
      ├── ProbabilityEngine
      ├── DecisionEngine
      ├── SignalGenerator
      ├── RiskManager
      └── ...

This is one complete system.

Tests folder

The tests/ folder is not another trading system.

Its job is only to verify that the production code behaves correctly.

For example:

tests/

test_market_structure.py

tests only:

MarketStructureEngine

It doesn't need to know anything about:

RiskManager
SignalGenerator
Backtesting

unless that specific test requires them.

Another example:

tests/

test_probability_engine.py

Only tests

ProbabilityEngine

It shouldn't call:

TradingPipeline

unless you're writing an integration test.

Think of tests as inspectors

Imagine your trading platform is a factory.

Factory

Machine A
Machine B
Machine C
Machine D

Then the tests are inspectors.

Inspector A

checks Machine A
Inspector B

checks Machine B

The inspectors don't report to each other.

They simply verify that each machine works.

There are different types of tests
1. Unit Tests

Example

tests/

test_bos_detector.py

Only tests

BOSDetector

Nothing else.

2. Integration Tests

These check that modules work together.

Example

MarketStructure
      ↓
FeatureEngineering
      ↓
Probability

This is allowed because it's testing integration.

3. End-to-End Tests

These run the whole pipeline.

Example

TradingPipeline
      ↓
Backtest

This verifies the complete system.

